WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.810
Suppose you write a loop

2
00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.030
run it on ten inputs

3
00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.940
and every answer is right.

4
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.130
Is the loop correct?

5
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.690
There are infinitely many possible inputs

6
00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.903
and ten tests cannot cover them.

7
00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.113
So how do you know a loop is

8
00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.150
correct for every input

9
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.160
not just the ones you tried?

10
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.891
The tool for this is the loop invariant

11
00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.111
and it is built on a proof technique

12
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.900
from discrete maths: induction.

13
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.960
In this video we'll review induction

14
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.020
see why a loop is induction in disguise

15
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.170
and use three questions to check any loop.

16
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.401
Induction proves a statement

17
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.980
about every whole number n, one, two, three

18
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.710
and so on, using just two steps.

19
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.788
First, the base case

20
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.199
show the statement is true

21
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.620
for the first value, n equals one.

22
00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.159
Second, the inductive step

23
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.063
show that whenever the statement

24
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.520
is true for some k

25
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.130
it is also true for k plus one.

26
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.790
Think of dominoes.

27
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.601
The base case pushes the first one over.

28
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.667
The inductive step says each falling

29
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.110
domino knocks over the next.

30
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.960
Together, every domino falls.

31
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.710
Let's prove a formula we will use again

32
00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.830
one plus two, up to n

33
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.260
equals n times n plus one, over two.

34
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.200
Base case: n equals one.

35
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.540
The left side is just one.

36
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.000
The right side is one times two over two

37
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.620
which is also one.

38
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.850
It holds.

39
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.400
Now the inductive step.

40
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.341
Assume the formula holds for some k.

41
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.063
That assumption is called the

42
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.110
inductive hypothesis.

43
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.550
Add the next number, k plus one

44
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.510
to both sides.

45
00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.130
The left side is now one plus up to k

46
00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.433
plus k plus one

47
00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.723
and by the hypothesis that equals

48
00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.630
k times k plus one over two

49
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.340
plus k plus one.

50
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.900
Factor out k plus one

51
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.908
and what is left is k plus

52
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.580
one times k plus two, over two.

53
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.280
That is exactly the formula for k plus one.

54
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.580
The base case holds and the step holds

55
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.810
so the formula is true for every n.

56
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:24.863
Now look at a loop.

57
00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.269
Each pass through the body

58
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:27.990
is like the next domino.

59
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.885
A loop invariant is a statement that is

60
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.630
true at the top of every iteration

61
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.580
just before the loop tests its condition.

62
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.380
To prove one, we ask three questions.

63
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.260
Initialization

64
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.420
is it true before the first iteration?

65
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.764
Maintenance: if it is true at the top of one

66
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.920
iteration and the body runs

67
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.640
is it true at the top of the next?

68
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.649
Termination: when the loop stops

69
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.651
do the invariant and the exit condition

70
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.370
together give us what we wanted?

71
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.840
Initialization is the base case.

72
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.562
Maintenance is the inductive step.

73
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.122
Termination is the extra step that connects

74
00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.130
the invariant to the answer.

75
00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.391
First example: finding the largest item

76
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.380
in a list of n items.

77
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.510
The loop keeps a variable called best.

78
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.170
The invariant

79
00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.570
at the top of the iteration for item i

80
00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:29.460
best is the largest of the first i items.

81
00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.180
Initialization

82
00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.660
best starts as the first item

83
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.160
which is the largest of a list of one.

84
00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.660
Maintenance: if the next item is bigger

85
00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.480
best is replaced by it.

86
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.980
If not, best stays.

87
00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.260
Either way

88
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.060
best is now the largest of one more item.

89
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:50.920
Termination

90
00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.560
the loop stops after the last item

91
00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.490
so best is the largest of all n.

92
00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.540
Second example: adding up one to n

93
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.510
with a variable called total.

94
00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:03.970
The invariant

95
00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.470
at the top of the iteration for i

96
00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.250
total equals one plus two

97
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.460
up to i minus one.

98
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.180
Initialization: i is one and total is zero

99
00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.080
the empty sum.

100
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.700
Maintenance: the body adds i

101
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.142
so total becomes one plus up to i

102
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.916
which is the invariant for

103
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.060
the next iteration.

104
00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.040
Termination

105
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.420
the loop ends with i equal to n plus one

106
00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.420
so total is one plus up to n

107
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.860
and that is n times n plus one over two.

108
00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.050
The formula we proved by induction.

109
00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.040
Notice what we did.

110
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.640
We never ran the loop on any input.

111
00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.950
We reasoned about every input at once.

112
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.430
Tests can only show that a bug exists.

113
00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:55.290
An invariant, argued by induction

114
00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.940
shows that a bug cannot exist.

115
00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.920
And when a loop is wrong

116
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.960
the three questions tell you where

117
00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.480
the start, the step, or the exit.

118
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.939
In the code, we will check an invariant

119
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.360
while the program runs

120
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.530
and watch it catch two wrong versions.

121
00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.465
To sum up.

122
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.355
Induction needs a base case

123
00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.230
and an inductive step.

124
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.852
A loop invariant is a statement that is

125
00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.700
true at the top of every iteration.

126
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.428
Initialization

127
00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.882
maintenance and termination are the three

128
00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.850
questions to ask of any loop.

129
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.847
Next, we will see these questions in code

130
00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.095
with assertions that check the invariant

131
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.600
while the loop runs.

132
00:05:36.227 --> 00:05:38.797
Now let's check invariants in Java

133
00:05:38.797 --> 00:05:41.827
with assertions that run inside the loop.

134
00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:45.164
Here is the largest-item loop in Java.

135
00:05:45.237 --> 00:05:47.370
The invariant sits at the top of

136
00:05:47.437 --> 00:05:49.636
the loop body as an assert

137
00:05:49.717 --> 00:05:51.665
best must equal the maximum

138
00:05:51.737 --> 00:05:53.397
of the first i items

139
00:05:53.397 --> 00:05:55.417
computed by a small helper.

140
00:05:55.417 --> 00:05:57.257
Then the loop does its real work

141
00:05:57.257 --> 00:05:59.137
if the next item is bigger

142
00:05:59.137 --> 00:06:00.327
best is replaced.

143
00:06:00.677 --> 00:06:02.287
If the assert fails

144
00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:04.184
Java throws an AssertionError

145
00:06:04.247 --> 00:06:06.147
that names the iteration.

146
00:06:06.147 --> 00:06:07.187
One detail

147
00:06:07.187 --> 00:06:10.018
Java switches assertions off by default.

148
00:06:10.087 --> 00:06:11.412
You must run the program

149
00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:13.364
with the dash e a flag.

150
00:06:13.447 --> 00:06:15.084
And since the helper scans

151
00:06:15.147 --> 00:06:16.987
the prefix every time

152
00:06:16.987 --> 00:06:19.047
assertions like this belong in tests

153
00:06:19.047 --> 00:06:20.397
not in production code.

154
00:06:20.977 --> 00:06:23.555
The summing loop has the same shape.

155
00:06:23.627 --> 00:06:26.051
Assert that total equals i minus

156
00:06:26.127 --> 00:06:28.527
one times i over two

157
00:06:28.527 --> 00:06:32.407
which is one plus up to i minus one.

158
00:06:32.407 --> 00:06:34.147
Then add i.

159
00:06:34.147 --> 00:06:35.427
After the loop

160
00:06:35.427 --> 00:06:37.427
a last assert checks the answer

161
00:06:37.427 --> 00:06:39.587
that is the termination question.

162
00:06:39.587 --> 00:06:42.007
Notice that total is a long

163
00:06:42.007 --> 00:06:44.697
so large sums do not overflow.

164
00:06:45.277 --> 00:06:47.401
Now a wrong version.

165
00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:51.630
It starts best at zero instead

166
00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:53.587
of at the first item.

167
00:06:53.587 --> 00:06:55.367
Look at the start.

168
00:06:55.367 --> 00:06:57.341
On the array three, one, four

169
00:06:57.407 --> 00:06:58.760
this method would even return

170
00:06:58.807 --> 00:07:00.357
the right answer, four.

171
00:07:00.707 --> 00:07:02.945
But the invariant is false from

172
00:07:03.017 --> 00:07:04.437
the very first check.

173
00:07:04.437 --> 00:07:07.397
At i equals one, best is zero

174
00:07:07.397 --> 00:07:10.997
and the largest of the first item is three.

175
00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:12.337
Initialization fails

176
00:07:12.337 --> 00:07:13.931
and the assert says so

177
00:07:13.997 --> 00:07:15.542
even though the output would

178
00:07:15.597 --> 00:07:16.707
have looked fine.

179
00:07:17.287 --> 00:07:19.037
Another wrong version.

180
00:07:19.117 --> 00:07:20.790
It compares the next item

181
00:07:20.857 --> 00:07:24.407
with the previous item, not with best.

182
00:07:24.757 --> 00:07:28.847
On five, one, two, zero: at i equals two

183
00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:30.779
the item two is bigger than

184
00:07:30.847 --> 00:07:34.087
its neighbour one, so best becomes two

185
00:07:34.087 --> 00:07:36.147
and the five is lost.

186
00:07:36.147 --> 00:07:38.115
Maintenance failed in that iteration.

187
00:07:38.167 --> 00:07:39.789
The assert catches it at the

188
00:07:39.847 --> 00:07:42.197
top of the next one, at i equals three.

189
00:07:42.777 --> 00:07:44.307
Here is the output.

190
00:07:44.307 --> 00:07:46.767
The correct methods answer five

191
00:07:46.767 --> 00:07:48.294
and five thousand and fifty.

192
00:07:48.347 --> 00:07:50.343
Both wrong versions are stopped

193
00:07:50.407 --> 00:07:51.507
by the invariant

194
00:07:51.507 --> 00:07:54.017
and the message names the iteration.

195
00:07:54.597 --> 00:07:56.647
An assert is still a test

196
00:07:56.647 --> 00:07:58.447
it checks the inputs you run.

197
00:07:58.507 --> 00:08:00.219
The argument by induction

198
00:08:00.287 --> 00:08:01.947
covers all of them.

199
00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:03.317
Use both.

200
00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:05.603
Ask the three questions of

201
00:08:05.677 --> 00:08:07.037
every loop you write.

202
00:08:07.037 --> 00:08:08.477
In the next chapter

203
00:08:08.477 --> 00:08:11.547
we will use them on arrays and linked lists.

